<h2>The dispute</h2>

<p>The appellant\'s son died in December 2016, and his two children continued living with their mother. The paternal grandfather petitioned the Family Court under Sections 7, 8, 9 and 10 read with Section 25 of the Guardians and Wards Act, 1890, seeking custody. His case rested on two propositions: that the mother, a working woman, could not give the children adequate care and a suitable environment, and that the grandparents could provide a better upbringing. The Family Court declined, and the grandfather appealed to the High Court under Section 19 of the Family Courts Act, 1984.</p>

<h2>What the Division Bench held</h2>

<p>The Bench of Justice Subramonium Prasad and Justice Renu Bhatnagar dismissed the appeal, describing the Family Court\'s order as well reasoned. The judgment proceeds along four lines.</p>

<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><h3>Hierarchy of guardianship</h3><p>The mother, as natural guardian, has a superior claim over grandparents; custody shifts only on proof that she is unfit, incapable or negligent — and nothing on record showed she was failing the children.</p></div>
  <div class="tile"><h3>Capacity, not gender roles</h3><p>Her financial independence as an Income-Tax Officer with her own accommodation answered the "working woman" objection rather than validating it.</p></div>
  <div class="tile"><h3>The children\'s voice</h3><p>At about 17 and 14, the children were capable of rational thought, expressed a wish to live with their mother, and showed no sign of having been coached.</p></div>
  <div class="tile"><h3>Scope discipline</h3><p>Allegations of cruelty against the appellant\'s late son were not examined — Guardians and Wards Act proceedings decide welfare and custody, not matrimonial fault.</p></div>
</div>

<p>On precedent, the Bench applied Vivek Kumar Chaturvedi v. State of U.P., (2025) 4 SCC 342, where the Supreme Court held that grandparents cannot assert a better claim than a natural guardian. The visitation question was handled with similar care: the grandfather may apply to the Family Court, which will decide after ascertaining the grandchildren\'s willingness; the elder grandchild, now a major, may visit if she chooses, and no court order can compel or forbid her.</p>

<h2>The welfare principle at work</h2>

<table class="law">
  <tr><th>Consideration</th><th>How it operated here</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Welfare of the minor (paramount)</td><td>Stable home with the mother for nearly a decade since the father\'s death; no evidence of neglect</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Natural guardianship</td><td>Mother\'s statutory position under the Hindu Minority and Guardianship framework preferred absent disqualification</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Child\'s preference</td><td>Ascertained directly by the Family Court through interaction; age-appropriate weight given</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Financial and residential stability</td><td>Mother\'s employment and accommodation treated as strengths</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Continuity</td><td>Disturbing a settled arrangement requires compelling cause, which was absent</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>Takeaways for families on both sides</h2>

<p>For grandparents, the realistic remedy after a child\'s death is almost always a visitation framework, not custody — and courts respond better to petitions that seek contact and involvement than to those that attack the surviving parent\'s fitness without concrete evidence. For surviving parents, the judgment underlines the value of the record: stable schooling, financial independence and the children\'s own settled routine answered every allegation. And for practitioners, two procedural notes: custody appeals from Family Court orders travel under Section 19 of the Family Courts Act within the limitation that provision prescribes, and custody courts will decline invitations to adjudicate collateral matrimonial grievances, which belong — if anywhere — to other proceedings.</p>

<div class="note"><p>This article summarises a Delhi High Court custody judgment for general information. Custody and guardianship outcomes turn on the welfare of the particular child on the particular record. Nothing here is legal advice.</p></div>
